00:07:01.330 --> 00:07:04.820
and see how to make that secure.

00:07:04.820 --> 00:07:06.850
So the first thing
is data encryption.

00:07:06.850 --> 00:07:09.790
So data encryption, here, we're
talking about data encryption

00:07:09.790 --> 00:07:11.010
in transit.

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:13.940
TLS is what most
people should be using.

00:07:13.940 --> 00:07:17.950
It's a very standard protocol.

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:20.380
It's used to connect
to your bank,

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:21.880
if you're familiar with that.

00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:23.190
I mean, TLS is very well known.

00:07:23.190 --> 00:07:24.190
It's very well deployed.

00:07:24.190 --> 00:07:25.794
It's been battle tested.

00:07:25.794 --> 00:07:27.460
This is something
that you should always

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:30.135
have between your device and
the Cloud Platform, the Google

00:07:30.135 --> 00:07:30.760
Cloud Platform.

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:34.780
You should be using TLS for
data encryption in transit.

00:07:34.780 --> 00:07:36.822
That's very important,
and that's a given.

00:07:36.822 --> 00:07:37.780
That's the first thing.

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:40.749
So they say, OK, we'll
use TLS to communicate

00:07:40.749 --> 00:07:42.790
between our device and
the Google Cloud Platform.

00:07:42.790 --> 00:07:43.900
Great.

00:07:43.900 --> 00:07:45.520
Now, how do you authenticate?

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:46.340
We have encryption.

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:48.970
We need to authenticate
those devices.

00:07:48.970 --> 00:07:52.180
There's two ways to authenticate
devices, if you wish.

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:54.160
There's kind of a
certificate-based

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:57.790
authentication and a
password-based authentication.

00:07:57.790 --> 00:08:01.180
Do not use password
based for devices.

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:03.520
Passwords are for human.

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:08.830
Password is an element that
somebody has in their head,

00:08:08.830 --> 00:08:10.659
and the head is
the secure storage.

00:08:10.659 --> 00:08:12.700
You're not supposed to
write it down in a Post-it

00:08:12.700 --> 00:08:13.880
and put it on your keyboard.

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:14.921
That's not how you do it.

00:08:14.921 --> 00:08:16.300
You keep it in your head.

00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:18.910
And then because I know the
password, I can authenticate.

00:08:18.910 --> 00:08:22.190
So I'm the secure element here.

00:08:22.190 --> 00:08:24.220
The problem with a password
is if somebody else

00:08:24.220 --> 00:08:28.300
knows your password,
that other person is you.

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:32.110
There's no validation that you
are the person you say you are.

00:08:32.110 --> 00:08:35.440
You're just showing a
password, and it says, I am me.

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:38.080
And that's why the secure
storage in your head

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:40.840
is very important is to
prove that it is you.

00:08:40.840 --> 00:08:42.669
That's the main issue
with a password.

00:08:42.669 --> 00:08:44.350
A password is you.

00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:47.530
With a certificate, there's
a certificate authority

00:08:47.530 --> 00:08:49.390
that validates that
what you're presenting

00:08:49.390 --> 00:08:51.490
as an identity is actually you.

00:08:51.490 --> 00:08:53.830
So that authority will say,
yes, yes, that public key,

00:08:53.830 --> 00:08:55.210
that certificate, great.

00:08:55.210 --> 00:08:58.600
That's device ABC,
and that's great.

00:08:58.600 --> 00:09:01.510
So you should always use
certificate for devices,

00:09:01.510 --> 00:09:04.860
because you don't have the
head as a secure storage,

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:06.037
as a human does.

00:09:06.037 --> 00:09:07.120
So this is very important.

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:10.560
And also, that third-party
validation of the identity

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:11.930
is very important.

00:09:11.930 --> 00:09:14.301
So use certificate for devices.

00:09:14.301 --> 00:09:14.800
Fine.

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:17.400
So Acme says, OK, we'll
use TLS for encryption.

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:20.790
We'll use certificate
for identity.

00:09:20.790 --> 00:09:22.710
But then I have certificates.

00:09:22.710 --> 00:09:25.320
So I have public keys
and private keys.

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:27.510
How do I give those
keys to my manufacturer?

00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:29.760
How do I put those
keys on my device?

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:33.030
And that's actually
a pretty big problem.

00:09:33.030 --> 00:09:34.840
There are several
ways you can do that.

00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:37.830
You can create a file
with all the keys

00:09:37.830 --> 00:09:40.200
and send that file
to your manufacturer.

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:42.117
That's one way to do it.

00:09:42.117 --> 00:09:44.200
You have to trust your
manufacturer at this point,

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:46.980
because the manufacturer
sees your keys,

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:49.110
and they can actually
copy those keys

00:09:49.110 --> 00:09:51.190
if they're malicious a bit.

00:09:51.190 --> 00:09:54.960
It does happen fairly
often that the manufacturer

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:57.880
will generate all your devices,
send your devices, fine.

00:09:57.880 --> 00:10:00.600
And then all of a sudden, you
see maybe 10% of those devices

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:04.490
are rogue devices, are fake
devices, but using valid keys.

00:10:04.490 --> 00:10:07.280
They're copies of existing keys,
because they were in a file,

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:08.280
and they've been copied.

00:10:08.280 --> 00:10:09.850
So that's a little
bit of an issue.

00:10:09.850 --> 00:10:12.730
So that's not really
a good way to do it.

00:10:12.730 --> 00:10:14.670
The other way is to
say, hey, manufacturer,

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:17.461
you're going to do an
API call every time

00:10:17.461 --> 00:10:19.710
you're going to flash that
firmware and my identities.

00:10:19.710 --> 00:10:21.840
You're going to do an
API call to a back end

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:24.450
to get the keys right on time.

00:10:24.450 --> 00:10:27.540
The manufacturers hate
that, because it slows down

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:29.410
the manufacturing process.

00:10:29.410 --> 00:10:32.100
So imagine that you have
called to get the key.

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:33.296
That's a round trip.

00:10:33.296 --> 00:10:34.545
They get to download that key.

00:10:34.545 --> 00:10:35.480
They have to flash it.

00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:36.979
And then they go
to the next device.

00:10:36.979 --> 00:10:37.890
That's fairly slow.

00:10:37.890 --> 00:10:40.610
And what happens sometimes
is that call doesn't work.

00:10:40.610 --> 00:10:42.130
That call's too slow.

00:10:42.130 --> 00:10:44.370
It's slowing down the
manufacturing process,

00:10:44.370 --> 00:10:46.420
which adds cost to you.

00:10:46.420 --> 00:10:48.180
So when they do that,
they will actually

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:51.960
charge you way more than just a
standard manufacturing process

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:53.370
that goes really fast.

00:10:53.370 --> 00:10:55.344
That's costing a lot of
money, and they usually

00:10:55.344 --> 00:10:56.010
don't like that.

00:10:56.010 --> 00:10:57.801
Actually, a lot of
manufacturers don't even

00:10:57.801 --> 00:11:01.160
have their manufacturing line
connected to the internet.

00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:03.060
They don't even
allow you to do this.

00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:05.610
So you revert to
giving them a file

00:11:05.610 --> 00:11:07.020
with the copies of the keys.

00:11:07.020 --> 00:11:09.070
So that's not great.

00:11:09.070 --> 00:11:13.830
So we need to find
a solution for that.

00:11:13.830 --> 00:11:15.610
TLS mutual authentication.

00:11:15.610 --> 00:11:17.650
So this is the authentication.

00:11:17.650 --> 00:11:19.380
We say what we're going to use.

00:11:19.380 --> 00:11:20.790
We're going to use certificates.

00:11:20.790 --> 00:11:22.020
Great.

00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:25.350
So the certificate can be used
to authenticate the cloud,

00:11:25.350 --> 00:11:27.510
like you would authenticate
with your bank.

00:11:27.510 --> 00:11:30.107
When you do that, you
know it's the bank,

00:11:30.107 --> 00:11:31.940
but the bank doesn't
know it's you until you

00:11:31.940 --> 00:11:33.030
put username, password.

00:11:33.030 --> 00:11:34.480
It's the same for device.

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:36.900
They can authenticate the cloud.

00:11:36.900 --> 00:11:39.390
The cloud needs to
authenticate the device,

00:11:39.390 --> 00:11:42.270
so it's a mutual
authentication both ways.

00:11:42.270 --> 00:11:44.910
And you can use TLS
mutual auth to do that.

00:11:44.910 --> 00:11:46.320
There's good and bad for it.

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:47.970
It's not all good.

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:50.300
So we'll look at that.

00:11:50.300 --> 00:11:51.550
And finally, secure boot.

00:11:51.550 --> 00:11:55.210
We want to make sure
that what the device is

00:11:55.210 --> 00:11:58.097
running when it boots is
what's supposed to be running.

00:11:58.097 --> 00:12:00.430
The first thing that a hacker
will do when they get onto

00:12:00.430 --> 00:12:03.550
a device, they'll modify the
firmware so the device cannot

00:12:03.550 --> 00:12:05.130
do what it's
supposed to be doing.

00:12:05.130 --> 00:12:06.430
Mirai was doing that.

00:12:06.430 --> 00:12:08.290
Mirai would get on
the device, shut

00:12:08.290 --> 00:12:10.420
off everything, every
other connection,

00:12:10.420 --> 00:12:11.750
and reconfigure itself.

00:12:11.750 --> 00:12:13.720
So nobody else could come in.

00:12:13.720 --> 00:12:15.610
Actually, it would shut
down any other Mirai

00:12:15.610 --> 00:12:16.900
attack, which was pretty good.

00:12:16.900 --> 00:12:19.700
But it would shut down
any access to the device.

00:12:19.700 --> 00:12:21.220
Then it would
reconfigure the device

00:12:21.220 --> 00:12:23.370
to do what it wanted it to do.

00:12:23.370 --> 00:12:26.340
So make sure that
when the device boots,

00:12:26.340 --> 00:12:28.210
there is a validation
of the firmware that

00:12:28.210 --> 00:12:29.500
says, yes, yes, it is right.

00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:30.634
It is the right firmware.

00:12:30.634 --> 00:12:32.300
And it's supposed to
boot the right way,

00:12:32.300 --> 00:12:33.591
and it's doing the right thing.

00:12:33.591 --> 00:12:34.800
So I let the device boot.

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:36.250
So that's secure boot.

00:12:36.250 --> 00:12:41.320
And that's really important
to implement in your device.

00:12:41.320 --> 00:12:44.020
And the key rotation is
something that is actually

00:12:44.020 --> 00:12:46.330
forgotten quite often.

00:12:46.330 --> 00:12:48.790
You need to think
more than a year away.

00:12:48.790 --> 00:12:51.790
No, think four years,
five, 10 years, 15 years.

00:12:51.790 --> 00:12:54.910
In manufacturing, we see
15, 20 years sometimes.

00:12:54.910 --> 00:12:57.670
Keys will rotate, will
have to be rotated.

00:12:57.670 --> 00:12:59.180
Sometimes, they're corrupted.

00:12:59.180 --> 00:13:02.530
There's been a hack in your
back end system, in your CA.

00:13:02.530 --> 00:13:04.150
The keys have been exposed.

00:13:04.150 --> 00:13:05.200
You have to rotate them.

00:13:05.200 --> 00:13:08.277
Maybe the CA has to be
rotated every few years.

00:13:08.277 --> 00:13:09.360
And that's very important.

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:10.770
That's hard to do.

00:13:10.770 --> 00:13:14.650
So you have to really consider
throughout your design

00:13:14.650 --> 00:13:18.320
that the keys are ephemeral
and will have to be changed.

00:13:18.320 --> 00:13:20.530
So that's something very
important to consider

00:13:20.530 --> 00:13:21.940
when you build your device.

00:13:21.940 --> 00:13:25.570
Think that your keys are
not going to be permanent

00:13:25.570 --> 00:13:27.250
and will have to be rotated.

00:13:27.250 --> 00:13:28.870
How does your
software and firmware

00:13:28.870 --> 00:13:33.040
manage that to make sure that
that's feasible over time?

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:37.667
That's a very important point
that's oftentimes overlooked.

00:13:37.667 --> 00:13:39.250
So if you look at
all the challenges--

00:13:39.250 --> 00:13:42.340
so you need certificates
and a certificate authority.

00:13:42.340 --> 00:13:44.590
And there's complexity
coming with that.

00:13:44.590 --> 00:13:46.949
The manufacturing cost
and risk associated

00:13:46.949 --> 00:13:49.240
with transferring the keys
to your manufacturer, that's

00:13:49.240 --> 00:13:50.580
a problem.

00:13:50.580 --> 00:13:55.630
TLS and TLS mutual auth is
actually a little bit loaded

00:13:55.630 --> 00:13:56.750
and hard to maintain.

00:13:56.750 --> 00:13:59.440
So we'll see how
we can solve this.

00:13:59.440 --> 00:14:04.730
Keys are extremely difficult
to store securely in firmware.

00:14:04.730 --> 00:14:08.510
If it's firmware or software,
somebody can have access to it.

00:14:08.510 --> 00:14:13.106
Somebody can reverse engineer
the code and decompile

00:14:13.106 --> 00:14:14.480
and can get access
to those keys.

00:14:14.480 --> 00:14:19.890
It's extremely hard to
secure any key in a firmware.

00:14:19.890 --> 00:14:21.360
You have to do
secure boot, and you

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:23.530
have to have key
rotation management.

00:14:23.530 --> 00:14:26.587
So those are six points that
are really big challenges.

00:14:26.587 --> 00:14:28.420
And at this point,
you're probably thinking,

00:14:28.420 --> 00:14:30.030
man, this is a problem.

00:14:30.030 --> 00:14:32.290
How am I going to
secure my IoT devices?

00:14:32.290 --> 00:14:33.855
This is too complicated.

00:14:33.855 --> 00:14:37.360
Well, let's see how
we can help with that.

00:14:37.360 --> 00:14:39.560
So let's talk a bit
about Google Cloud IoT.

00:14:39.560 --> 00:14:43.380
The Google Cloud IoT Platform
is a set of services.

00:14:43.380 --> 00:14:46.440
One of the most recent
is called Cloud IoT Core.

00:14:46.440 --> 00:14:48.970
It's the big one
on the left here.

00:14:48.970 --> 00:14:51.750
Cloud IoT Core has two
components-- a device manager,

00:14:51.750 --> 00:14:54.160
where you'll declare your
devices, your attributes,

00:14:54.160 --> 00:14:57.090
your registries, your access
controls, all of that,

00:14:57.090 --> 00:14:57.750
and your keys.

00:14:57.750 --> 00:15:00.600
You're going to store your
keys in there, the public keys.

00:15:00.600 --> 00:15:05.130
And then you have a
data bridge or broker

00:15:05.130 --> 00:15:08.160
for entry TTN and HTTP
to be able to communicate

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:12.570
bidirectionally with the
devices and between the devices

00:15:12.570 --> 00:15:13.680
and the cloud.

00:15:13.680 --> 00:15:15.585
All the data coming
into Cloud IoT Core

00:15:15.585 --> 00:15:20.460
is passed on to Cloud Pub/Sub,
a globally available messaging

00:15:20.460 --> 00:15:21.240
service.

00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:23.280
From Pub/Sub, you can
tree your Cloud functions,

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:25.500
serverless functions,
and compute.

00:15:25.500 --> 00:15:28.890
You can trigger data flows
that can do streaming analytics

00:15:28.890 --> 00:15:30.240
over a certain period of time.

00:15:30.240 --> 00:15:33.200
You can do transformation,
any kind of operation

00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:34.860
at very high scale.

00:15:34.860 --> 00:15:39.390
From there, you'll go into
storage in BigTable, BigQuery,

00:15:39.390 --> 00:15:43.950
maybe Firestore, and trigger
Cloud ML training, if needed.

00:15:43.950 --> 00:15:47.220
Those trainings could be used
by data flow or Cloud functions

00:15:47.220 --> 00:15:52.860
to do inferences and then pass
back to the device eventually.

00:15:52.860 --> 00:15:56.100
On the far right, Data Studio
and Datalab and analytics

00:15:56.100 --> 00:15:58.140
allow you to visualize data.

00:15:58.140 --> 00:16:00.190
Silly little time series.

00:16:00.190 --> 00:16:03.150
See any information you
need about your data coming

00:16:03.150 --> 00:16:04.800
from the devices.

00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:06.930
That's our Cloud IoT platform.

00:16:06.930 --> 00:16:09.120
It's a fully managed platform.

00:16:09.120 --> 00:16:12.300
Cloud IoT Core is not
installed or deployed

00:16:12.300 --> 00:16:15.850
or configured in terms of
shard size or anything.

00:16:15.850 --> 00:16:17.160
It's a global service.

00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:19.960
It will be accessible
anywhere in the world.

00:16:19.960 --> 00:16:21.780
And it's scalable for you.

00:16:21.780 --> 00:16:24.386
One device, million device,
doesn't really matter.

00:16:24.386 --> 00:16:26.010
You just throw whatever
you have at it.

00:16:26.010 --> 00:16:27.634
It will scale up and
down to your need.

00:16:31.000 --> 00:16:32.230
So how do we secure all that?

00:16:32.230 --> 00:16:36.250
Step one, use a secure element.

00:16:36.250 --> 00:16:37.520
Use a crypto chip.

00:16:37.520 --> 00:16:39.620
This is super important.

00:16:39.620 --> 00:16:42.190
I cannot emphasize that enough.

00:16:42.190 --> 00:16:45.430
You should use a crypto chip
in your device if you can.

00:16:45.430 --> 00:16:48.990
The private keys from secure
chips are never exposed.

00:16:48.990 --> 00:16:51.206
They are generated
inside the hardware.

00:16:51.206 --> 00:16:53.080
Private key is generated
inside the hardware.

00:16:53.080 --> 00:16:55.690
The public key is
generated and then exposed

00:16:55.690 --> 00:16:57.790
so it can be distributed
to the back end.

00:16:57.790 --> 00:16:58.910
This is super important.

00:16:58.910 --> 00:17:01.540
We have a great partnership
with Microchip here on that.

00:17:01.540 --> 00:17:03.190
You're talking subdollar.

00:17:03.190 --> 00:17:04.760
It's really cheap to add.

00:17:04.760 --> 00:17:06.970
And it saves you
a ton of trouble.

00:17:06.970 --> 00:17:09.940
You can send that
crypto pre-configured

00:17:09.940 --> 00:17:13.000
to your manufacturer
anywhere in the world,

00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:14.391
even if you don't trust them.

00:17:14.391 --> 00:17:16.390
Doesn't matter, because
they can't read anything

00:17:16.390 --> 00:17:17.015
from that chip.

00:17:17.015 --> 00:17:19.825
They'll just be able to
solder it to your PCB design,

00:17:19.825 --> 00:17:20.950
and then you're good to go.

00:17:20.950 --> 00:17:23.720
The identity has been
burned onto the device.

00:17:23.720 --> 00:17:25.359
It's super secure.

00:17:25.359 --> 00:17:28.290
There's really no
copying of keys possible.

00:17:28.290 --> 00:17:31.450
There's no API calls that
the manufacturer has to do.

00:17:31.450 --> 00:17:32.690
There's no constraint.

00:17:32.690 --> 00:17:36.280
So the cost of manufacturing
is actually much, much cheaper.

00:17:36.280 --> 00:17:39.592
And it oftentimes compensates
for the price of the chip.

00:17:39.592 --> 00:17:40.550
That's super important.

00:17:40.550 --> 00:17:45.280
I've seen customers
having to add $3 or $4

00:17:45.280 --> 00:17:48.125
to do custom manufacturing
for each of the devices.

00:17:48.125 --> 00:17:50.710
If you use this,
it's no more custom.

00:17:50.710 --> 00:17:51.550
It's a single thing.

00:17:51.550 --> 00:17:55.920
So you'd save $3 or $4 for a
chip that costs less than $1.

00:17:55.920 --> 00:17:57.970
So that's a very
good value prop.

00:17:57.970 --> 00:18:00.600
You should really
use a crypto chip.

00:18:00.600 --> 00:18:02.860
The crypto chip can also
do secure boot, by the way.

00:18:02.860 --> 00:18:07.150
So you can have the signature of
your firmware inside the crypto

00:18:07.150 --> 00:18:07.780
chip.

00:18:07.780 --> 00:18:11.440
When the device boots,
there's a hash that's

00:18:11.440 --> 00:18:12.610
calculated of your firmware.

00:18:12.610 --> 00:18:13.693
It's passed to the crypto.

00:18:13.693 --> 00:18:15.190
The crypto can say yes or no.

00:18:15.190 --> 00:18:16.820
And then the device
can boot or not.

00:18:16.820 --> 00:18:19.080
So you're doing
secure provisioning

00:18:19.080 --> 00:18:23.430
of keys, cheaper manufacturing
and more secure manufacturing.

00:18:23.430 --> 00:18:26.047
You're doing secure
boot with this.

00:18:26.047 --> 00:18:27.755
And also, we'll see,
you'll be able to do

00:18:27.755 --> 00:18:28.740
key rotation with this.

00:18:28.740 --> 00:18:30.550
So this is very important.

00:18:30.550 --> 00:18:34.010
Even if you feel it's a little
bit expensive, please do it.

00:18:34.010 --> 00:18:35.360
It will save you a ton of time.

00:18:35.360 --> 00:18:37.960
It will save you a lot
of trouble down the line.

00:18:37.960 --> 00:18:41.220
So use a secure [INAUDIBLE].

00:18:41.220 --> 00:18:42.920
Second, the authentication.

00:18:42.920 --> 00:18:46.675
I was talking about
mutual auth from TLS.

00:18:46.675 --> 00:18:48.050
Mutual auth is
great, but there's

00:18:48.050 --> 00:18:50.360
kind of a lot of downside to it.

00:18:50.360 --> 00:18:53.620
So we recommend using a
JWT for authentication.

00:18:53.620 --> 00:18:57.530
JWT is something that's used
by web services in general.

00:18:57.530 --> 00:18:58.940
And then it's a standard.

00:18:58.940 --> 00:19:02.660
It's a JSON web token.

00:19:02.660 --> 00:19:06.140
The way it works-- and I have
a little schematic after that--

00:19:06.140 --> 00:19:09.090
you establish a TLS session
with the Google front end

00:19:09.090 --> 00:19:10.200
anywhere in the world.

00:19:10.200 --> 00:19:12.185
As I said, Clout IoT
Core is a global service.

00:19:12.185 --> 00:19:15.150
So anywhere in the world,
you'll hit that Google front end

00:19:15.150 --> 00:19:15.987
with a TLS session.

00:19:15.987 --> 00:19:18.320
You'll establish that TLS
session with Google front end.

00:19:18.320 --> 00:19:18.890
Great.

00:19:18.890 --> 00:19:20.430
No problem.

00:19:20.430 --> 00:19:23.970
Then you're going to establish
your MQTT session over TLS.

00:19:23.970 --> 00:19:26.510
To do that, you're going to
use username and password

00:19:26.510 --> 00:19:27.380
from MQTT.

00:19:27.380 --> 00:19:30.000
So you're not using the
mutual auth at this point.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:31.640
You've established
your TLS session.

00:19:31.640 --> 00:19:32.700
That's it.

00:19:32.700 --> 00:19:35.840
But MQTT session has
username and password.

00:19:35.840 --> 00:19:39.500
The CONNECT statement of the
MQTT has username and password.

00:19:39.500 --> 00:19:40.865
Username is actually not used.

00:19:40.865 --> 00:19:42.740
You can put anything
you want in there-- say,

00:19:42.740 --> 00:19:44.270
"ignore" or "empty."

00:19:44.270 --> 00:19:45.500
Doesn't matter.

00:19:45.500 --> 00:19:48.440
The password is
actually not a password.

00:19:48.440 --> 00:19:51.740
It's a JWT token,
a JSON web token,

00:19:51.740 --> 00:19:54.950
that has a bunch of information
about the device, the device

00:19:54.950 --> 00:19:58.670
ID, all of that, that is
signed by the private key,

00:19:58.670 --> 00:20:02.847
so encrypted and signed by
the private key of the device.

00:20:02.847 --> 00:20:04.680
With the little crypto
I showed you earlier,

00:20:04.680 --> 00:20:07.130
that crypto is capable
of signing that JWT.

00:20:07.130 --> 00:20:09.470
All the APIs are able to
do it with that crypto.

00:20:09.470 --> 00:20:10.970
So it's very easy to do.

00:20:10.970 --> 00:20:12.740
You sign that JWT
with your private key,

00:20:12.740 --> 00:20:15.020
and you pass it
as your password.

00:20:15.020 --> 00:20:17.000
That JWT will be
valid from whatever

00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:19.430
you want to 24 hours, maximum.

00:20:19.430 --> 00:20:23.990
So you will have to rotate
every 24 hours at minimum.

00:20:23.990 --> 00:20:28.210
So that JWT is signed
and passed to IoT Core.

00:20:28.210 --> 00:20:30.800
IoT Core will look at the device
ID, will find the public key,

00:20:30.800 --> 00:20:35.120
and validate that the
signature from the private key

00:20:35.120 --> 00:20:36.590
is actually valid.

00:20:36.590 --> 00:20:37.520
And here you go.

00:20:37.520 --> 00:20:39.620
You have your device
authentication.

00:20:39.620 --> 00:20:43.070
So using that system, you
get mutual authentication

00:20:43.070 --> 00:20:46.760
from both cloud and device
without using the TLS

00:20:46.760 --> 00:20:48.480
mutual auth feature.

00:20:48.480 --> 00:20:52.850
We support ECC and
RSA on IoT Core.

00:20:52.850 --> 00:20:57.810
And that crypto that I showed
you can do both, as well.

00:20:57.810 --> 00:20:58.830
Little schematic.

00:20:58.830 --> 00:20:59.880
Bring it to life.

00:20:59.880 --> 00:21:01.410
We have the provisioner.

00:21:01.410 --> 00:21:04.100
The provisioner is a
person, is a company

00:21:04.100 --> 00:21:09.300
who will buy the
chips from Microchip.

00:21:09.300 --> 00:21:11.010
Microchip will
configure those keys

00:21:11.010 --> 00:21:13.197
with your CA and
your certificates.

00:21:13.197 --> 00:21:14.280
The private key is inside.

00:21:14.280 --> 00:21:15.960
The public key is passed to you.

00:21:15.960 --> 00:21:18.300
The provisioner
will run that script

00:21:18.300 --> 00:21:21.300
to put all the public
keys in Device Manager.

00:21:21.300 --> 00:21:22.930
All the public
keys are put there.

00:21:22.930 --> 00:21:24.305
The devices are
not even created,

00:21:24.305 --> 00:21:26.280
but all the public
keys are already there.

00:21:26.280 --> 00:21:30.250
The crypto is passed on
the device, burned on it.

00:21:30.250 --> 00:21:33.690
The device wakes up for the
first time, creates the JWT,

00:21:33.690 --> 00:21:37.590
signs the JWT with a crypto,
connects to the broker.

00:21:37.590 --> 00:21:38.790
The JWT is verified.

00:21:38.790 --> 00:21:41.160
We already have the
public keys there.

00:21:41.160 --> 00:21:42.580
We're all good to go.

00:21:42.580 --> 00:21:44.330
It works.

00:21:44.330 --> 00:21:49.850
So basically, doing that, you've
solved all of your problems.

00:21:49.850 --> 00:21:51.980
So it's really nice.

00:21:51.980 --> 00:21:53.990
So why not TLS mutual auth?

00:21:53.990 --> 00:21:56.570
Why using JWT instead?

00:21:56.570 --> 00:21:58.760
First thing is you achieve
mutual authentication

00:21:58.760 --> 00:22:01.270
with JWT and TLS both.

00:22:01.270 --> 00:22:05.990
So you achieve the same level
of authentication and security

00:22:05.990 --> 00:22:08.010
with both solutions.

00:22:08.010 --> 00:22:11.500
The benefit of the TLS
stack is much smaller.

00:22:11.500 --> 00:22:14.660
All you need is to establish
the TLS session with the cloud.

00:22:14.660 --> 00:22:15.190
That's all.

00:22:15.190 --> 00:22:18.160
You don't need to
use the mutual auth

00:22:18.160 --> 00:22:21.040
section of that stack, which
makes that stack much, much

00:22:21.040 --> 00:22:21.950
smaller.

00:22:21.950 --> 00:22:25.510
For example, we've
gone from 150 to 100k,

00:22:25.510 --> 00:22:28.277
roughly, to 10k for
the entire stack.

00:22:28.277 --> 00:22:29.860
So that's a big
difference, especially

00:22:29.860 --> 00:22:33.610
for devices that oftentimes have
32k of memory or 64k of memory.

00:22:33.610 --> 00:22:35.350
They can't put a
full TLS stack in it.

00:22:35.350 --> 00:22:37.430
You have to use a
different solution.

00:22:37.430 --> 00:22:38.710
This is what it brings you.

00:22:38.710 --> 00:22:41.170
You can bring a
much smaller stack.

00:22:41.170 --> 00:22:42.760
The speed is faster,
because you don't

00:22:42.760 --> 00:22:45.580
have to do that whole
handshake in mutual auth

00:22:45.580 --> 00:22:47.470
every time you establish
the TLS session.

00:22:47.470 --> 00:22:49.120
All you have to do is one TLS--

00:22:49.120 --> 00:22:49.730
boom.

00:22:49.730 --> 00:22:50.530
Username, password.

00:22:50.530 --> 00:22:51.030
You're done.

00:22:51.030 --> 00:22:54.100
It's much faster, less
consuming in bandwidth.

00:22:54.100 --> 00:22:56.810
There's no dependency on
the TLS stack you use.

00:22:56.810 --> 00:23:00.820
You do not have to use a special
TLS stack that's optimized,

00:23:00.820 --> 00:23:03.640
that has mutual auth
optimized and all of that.

00:23:03.640 --> 00:23:05.170
You can use any TLS
stack you want--

00:23:05.170 --> 00:23:06.790
open-source ones are very good--

00:23:06.790 --> 00:23:08.830
because the only thing
you use is the TLS

00:23:08.830 --> 00:23:10.570
session with your cloud.

00:23:10.570 --> 00:23:12.010
So that's less dependence.

00:23:12.010 --> 00:23:15.190
If you have to update the
TLS stack at some point--

00:23:15.190 --> 00:23:17.860
from 1.2 to 1.3, for example--

00:23:17.860 --> 00:23:20.770
your code is not dependent
on that TLS stack.

00:23:20.770 --> 00:23:22.390
The fact that you
use a crypto is not

00:23:22.390 --> 00:23:23.660
dependent on that TLS stack.

00:23:23.660 --> 00:23:28.600
So the upgrade of the TLS stack
is much easier, much less risk,

00:23:28.600 --> 00:23:31.570
much less testing to be done.

00:23:31.570 --> 00:23:34.030
Supports crypto, because
your code does it,

00:23:34.030 --> 00:23:35.360
not the TLS stack.

00:23:35.360 --> 00:23:37.260
No dependency, again.

00:23:37.260 --> 00:23:39.370
The ease of evolution
of the stack.

00:23:39.370 --> 00:23:41.719
As I said, there's no
validation of your code.

00:23:41.719 --> 00:23:42.760
Your code doesn't change.

00:23:42.760 --> 00:23:44.170
You can change the TLS
stack all you want.

00:23:44.170 --> 00:23:45.110
Your code's going
to be the same.

00:23:45.110 --> 00:23:47.068
It's going to generate
the JWT, sign it, put it

00:23:47.068 --> 00:23:48.700
as a password in
the MQTT CONNECT.

00:23:48.700 --> 00:23:50.170
Very easy.

00:23:50.170 --> 00:23:53.520
And then how do you support
multiple certificate here?

00:23:53.520 --> 00:23:55.135
With Cloud IoT
Core, you can use up

00:23:55.135 --> 00:24:00.490
to three certificates, so three
public keys per device, valley

00:24:00.490 --> 00:24:01.580
public keys per device.

00:24:01.580 --> 00:24:04.114
So what you'll do is you
have your public key.

00:24:04.114 --> 00:24:05.030
You want to rotate it.

00:24:05.030 --> 00:24:06.450
You'll create a
second public key.

00:24:06.450 --> 00:24:08.330
You'll put it there.

00:24:08.330 --> 00:24:09.535
You will re-authenticate.

00:24:09.535 --> 00:24:11.410
You will also [INAUDIBLE]
with the first one.

00:24:11.410 --> 00:24:16.289
Download all the information you
need for using the second key,

00:24:16.289 --> 00:24:18.580
then rotate, because both
can be used at the same time.

00:24:18.580 --> 00:24:20.746
And then you'll
invalidate the first one.

00:24:20.746 --> 00:24:22.120
So you could do
those rotation up

00:24:22.120 --> 00:24:23.820
to three keys at the same time.

00:24:23.820 --> 00:24:26.270
And you could do those
rotations very easily.

00:24:26.270 --> 00:24:28.956
It's much harder to do with the
mutual auth, because the TLS

00:24:28.956 --> 00:24:30.830
stack has to know where
the certificates are,

00:24:30.830 --> 00:24:32.246
et cetera, because
you are, again,

00:24:32.246 --> 00:24:34.570
dependent on the TLS stack.

00:24:34.570 --> 00:24:39.370
So by using the JWT token and
the signature with IoT core,

00:24:39.370 --> 00:24:41.860
you're simplifying
your life a whole bunch

00:24:41.860 --> 00:24:43.460
versus TLS mutual auth.

00:24:46.510 --> 00:24:49.300
For bigger devices, there's
an added layer of security

00:24:49.300 --> 00:24:50.530
that you can use.

00:24:50.530 --> 00:24:53.380
For MCU class devices,
you'll have to use whatever--

00:24:53.380 --> 00:24:56.820
Mongoose OS or Express
Logic or any of the--

00:24:56.820 --> 00:24:59.110
Zephyr from the Unix foundation.

00:24:59.110 --> 00:25:00.730
You should have
used some of those.

00:25:00.730 --> 00:25:06.010
But for larger devices that
have CPU class processors,

00:25:06.010 --> 00:25:07.820
you can use Android Things.

00:25:07.820 --> 00:25:12.530
Android Things is the optimized
version of Android 4 devices.

00:25:12.530 --> 00:25:14.830
It is a secure OS
from the ground up.

00:25:14.830 --> 00:25:16.390
It is a full OS.

00:25:16.390 --> 00:25:22.510
We, as the Google, will provide
secure patches, OS updates,

00:25:22.510 --> 00:25:24.537
like we would do on your phone.

00:25:24.537 --> 00:25:26.870
If you have an Android phone,
you get all those updates.

00:25:26.870 --> 00:25:28.560
You don't have to
really think about it

00:25:28.560 --> 00:25:30.550
versus if you have a
Linux server, for example,

00:25:30.550 --> 00:25:31.050
in the cloud.

00:25:31.050 --> 00:25:32.508
And when updates
are available, you

00:25:32.508 --> 00:25:35.950
have to go patch and update and
maintain and test all of that.

00:25:35.950 --> 00:25:38.380
We do all that for you
with Android Things.

00:25:38.380 --> 00:25:43.330
You do not have to manage
those environments on your own.

00:25:43.330 --> 00:25:46.450
Google will provide you the
firmware update, the OS update,

00:25:46.450 --> 00:25:48.910
the security patches,
and the infrastructure

00:25:48.910 --> 00:25:52.520
to deploy those
patches to the devices

00:25:52.520 --> 00:25:55.870
through the same back end
that actually deploys updates

00:25:55.870 --> 00:25:58.010
to your Android phones.

00:25:58.010 --> 00:26:01.090
It's a very
battle-tested back end.

00:26:01.090 --> 00:26:04.690
Those update processes have
been used millions, hundreds

00:26:04.690 --> 00:26:06.580
of millions of times already.

00:26:06.580 --> 00:26:10.000
It's a very secure way to send
those updates, very secure way

00:26:10.000 --> 00:26:12.310
to have an OS that you
don't have to worry about.

00:26:12.310 --> 00:26:15.790
Other benefits-- you can use
Android developers to build

00:26:15.790 --> 00:26:16.900
apps on Android Things.

00:26:16.900 --> 00:26:19.760
It's the same IDE, same
language, same everything.

00:26:19.760 --> 00:26:21.970
It's really the same
as an Android device.

00:26:21.970 --> 00:26:24.910
Very easy to deploy
an application.

00:26:24.910 --> 00:26:27.646
You can run TensorFlow models.

00:26:27.646 --> 00:26:31.300
on Android things, so run
machine learning, intelligence,

00:26:31.300 --> 00:26:32.390
on the edge.

00:26:32.390 --> 00:26:36.160
And you also have the Cloud IoT
connectivity as part of the OS.

00:26:36.160 --> 00:26:38.590
So you can communicate with
Cloud IoT Core and the Cloud

00:26:38.590 --> 00:26:42.930
IoT platform very easily
by using Android Things.

00:26:42.930 --> 00:26:44.710
So it's a good option.

00:26:44.710 --> 00:26:46.180
The only downside
of Android Things

00:26:46.180 --> 00:26:50.580
is you have to use certified
SOMs, so system and modules,

00:26:50.580 --> 00:26:54.754
certified hardware, that we
have tested and validated

00:26:54.754 --> 00:26:55.920
to work with Android Things.

00:26:55.920 --> 00:27:01.320
So we have a relationship with
Qualcomm, with NXP, with Arm.

00:27:01.320 --> 00:27:03.540
I believe some others.

00:27:03.540 --> 00:27:06.160
Can't remember
exactly the full list.

00:27:06.160 --> 00:27:08.780
But we have a bunch of modules
like that that you can use.

00:27:08.780 --> 00:27:13.170
And those are the only
approved hardware-- or some--

00:27:13.170 --> 00:27:14.694
that can be used
by Android Things.

00:27:14.694 --> 00:27:15.860
So that's the only downside.

00:27:15.860 --> 00:27:18.060
So if it's possible for
you to choose one of those,

00:27:18.060 --> 00:27:19.435
Android Things is
a great option.

00:27:19.435 --> 00:27:23.000
Otherwise, you'd have to
revert to something else.

00:27:23.000 --> 00:27:24.300
And I believe that's about it.

00:27:24.300 --> 00:27:27.140
If you want to know
more about Cloud IoT,

00:27:27.140 --> 00:27:32.540
you can go on that simple
URL, cloud.google.com/iot.

00:27:32.540 --> 00:27:34.980
You'll find sample code.

00:27:34.980 --> 00:27:37.560
You'll find example
documentation,

00:27:37.560 --> 00:27:39.840
a little bit of an overview
of the infrastructure.

00:27:39.840 --> 00:27:41.520
I invite you to come to Next.

00:27:41.520 --> 00:27:42.640
It's in a couple of weeks.

00:27:42.640 --> 00:27:44.306
That's something you
really want to see.

00:27:44.306 --> 00:27:46.900
There's going to be a lot
of IoT talks over there.

00:27:46.900 --> 00:27:48.460
And that's about it.

00:27:48.460 --> 00:27:52.040
So stay tuned for the life Q&amp;A.
We'll be back in less than a

00:27:52.040 --> 00:27:52.540
minute.

00:29:08.050 --> 00:29:12.220
Hey, welcome back for the
Q&amp;A around security and IoT.

00:29:12.220 --> 00:29:14.410
So we have a few questions
from the audience

00:29:14.410 --> 00:29:16.729
here, so let's look at that.

00:29:16.729 --> 00:29:18.520
I like the use of a
hardware root of trust,

00:29:18.520 --> 00:29:20.650
but many of our
customers don't have

00:29:20.650 --> 00:29:22.440
that in their current design.

00:29:22.440 --> 00:29:24.039
What can they do?

00:29:24.039 --> 00:29:26.080
That's a good question,
because I was emphasizing

00:29:26.080 --> 00:29:28.310
the use of hardware.

00:29:28.310 --> 00:29:28.960
And it's true.

00:29:28.960 --> 00:29:30.418
There's a lot of
hardware out there

00:29:30.418 --> 00:29:31.840
that don't have a root of trust.

00:29:31.840 --> 00:29:33.710
So how do we handle that?

00:29:33.710 --> 00:29:36.640
Because at first, you don't
have to use the root of trust.

00:29:36.640 --> 00:29:38.060
You can still
connect to IoT Core

00:29:38.060 --> 00:29:40.240
and benefit from the
JWT authentication,

00:29:40.240 --> 00:29:42.640
from the independence
on the TLS stack

00:29:42.640 --> 00:29:44.580
and all the security
of the back end.

00:29:44.580 --> 00:29:45.730
So let's put it that way.

00:29:45.730 --> 00:29:48.970
You can still use Cloud IoT
Core without the root of trust.

00:29:48.970 --> 00:29:50.660
That's totally fine.

00:29:50.660 --> 00:29:53.202
If you don't have one but you
want to add one to your design,

00:29:53.202 --> 00:29:54.534
there's several ways to do that.

00:29:54.534 --> 00:29:56.800
Those little cryptos I showed
you, the Microchip one,

00:29:56.800 --> 00:29:58.990
for example, use
I2C as a protocol

00:29:58.990 --> 00:30:04.300
to communicate with the rest
of the board, the PCB it's on.

00:30:04.300 --> 00:30:06.790
I2C is very common
on most devices,

00:30:06.790 --> 00:30:09.280
and you'll find a lot
of retrofit hardware

00:30:09.280 --> 00:30:14.200
that you can add to an
I2C bus on the hardware,

00:30:14.200 --> 00:30:16.310
either through a hat
that you plug in.

00:30:16.310 --> 00:30:19.330
There's a nice hat that
exists for pies, for example.

00:30:19.330 --> 00:30:20.550
As an example, put a hat.

00:30:20.550 --> 00:30:22.330
It has the crypto on it.

00:30:22.330 --> 00:30:24.730
You can use that
to retrofit design.

00:30:24.730 --> 00:30:27.430
And the cool thing about
I2C is you can do it hot,

00:30:27.430 --> 00:30:29.189
so you don't have to
shut down the device.

00:30:29.189 --> 00:30:29.980
That's pretty cool.

00:30:29.980 --> 00:30:33.070
You can just add that
crypto to existing devices.

00:30:33.070 --> 00:30:34.600
Other customers
I've seen doing is

00:30:34.600 --> 00:30:36.629
they use communication chips.

00:30:36.629 --> 00:30:38.920
So for example, you have a
device that's not connected,

00:30:38.920 --> 00:30:43.690
like in manufacturing, a PLC
on a manufacturing line that is

00:30:43.690 --> 00:30:45.170
not connected to the internet.

00:30:45.170 --> 00:30:46.720
They'll add a
communication module.

00:30:46.720 --> 00:30:48.430
And inside the
communication module,

00:30:48.430 --> 00:30:51.910
like a Wi-Fi or ethernet, you'll
have a crypto already in there

00:30:51.910 --> 00:30:53.500
that you can start using.

00:30:53.500 --> 00:30:55.060
And actually, the
encryption is all

00:30:55.060 --> 00:31:00.190
dealt by the communication
devices itself, not the MCU

00:31:00.190 --> 00:31:00.920
or the CPU.

00:31:00.920 --> 00:31:03.220
So that's a great
way to also retrofit

00:31:03.220 --> 00:31:05.140
without touching
the existing design.

00:31:05.140 --> 00:31:08.470
Just add the communication
chip, Wi-Fi, ethernet, whatever,

00:31:08.470 --> 00:31:11.440
serial, and then
add the crypto that

00:31:11.440 --> 00:31:12.720
has the crypto embedded in it.

00:31:12.720 --> 00:31:14.620
So there are ways to do that.

00:31:14.620 --> 00:31:17.890
It's mostly a retrofit
world, but it's

00:31:17.890 --> 00:31:20.380
easy to retrofit because
of the I2C communication.

00:31:20.380 --> 00:31:23.530
That's very standard.

00:31:23.530 --> 00:31:25.170
Another question.

00:31:25.170 --> 00:31:29.870
It still seems like the best way
to secure devices is changing.

00:31:29.870 --> 00:31:31.720
Changing devices, I assume.

00:31:31.720 --> 00:31:34.660
Should we wait until--

00:31:34.660 --> 00:31:36.460
is there any reason
for the customers

00:31:36.460 --> 00:31:39.950
to hold off any longer?

00:31:39.950 --> 00:31:45.130
So I think the question
here is more around,

00:31:45.130 --> 00:31:46.944
we need to change
everything to be secure.

00:31:46.944 --> 00:31:48.110
But I don't think that's OK.

00:31:48.110 --> 00:31:49.870
So I think you
shouldn't hold off.

00:31:49.870 --> 00:31:51.940
I think if you're
doing a design today,

00:31:51.940 --> 00:31:53.530
if you put a hardware
root of trust

00:31:53.530 --> 00:31:55.410
on it, if you put
a little crypto,

00:31:55.410 --> 00:31:57.070
you're basically future proof.

00:31:57.070 --> 00:31:59.390
You'll be able to update
that hardware root of trust

00:31:59.390 --> 00:32:00.082
if you need.

00:32:00.082 --> 00:32:02.290
You'll be able to rely on
that hardware root of trust

00:32:02.290 --> 00:32:04.960
to do a lot of
security improvement,

00:32:04.960 --> 00:32:07.660
key rotation, new
generation of certificates,

00:32:07.660 --> 00:32:11.290
validation of boot firmware,
images, and all of that.

00:32:11.290 --> 00:32:13.200
So there's no reason to wait.

00:32:13.200 --> 00:32:16.960
I think, overall, the
security is there.

00:32:16.960 --> 00:32:18.860
It's a matter of
implementing it.

00:32:18.860 --> 00:32:20.920
And we've seen that it
could be complicated,

00:32:20.920 --> 00:32:22.735
but with a solution
with IoT Core,

00:32:22.735 --> 00:32:25.360
with hardware root of trust and
the partnership with Microchip,

00:32:25.360 --> 00:32:28.690
for example, those
things are very much

00:32:28.690 --> 00:32:32.050
solved and various solutions
for security today.

00:32:32.050 --> 00:32:33.670
And you shouldn't wait.

00:32:33.670 --> 00:32:37.089
You can be secure
pretty easily today

00:32:37.089 --> 00:32:38.630
with the solutions
we have out there.

00:32:38.630 --> 00:32:41.830
So there's no reason to wait.

00:32:41.830 --> 00:32:44.780
In terms of price,
you said sub $1.

00:32:44.780 --> 00:32:46.870
This is still very
expensive for small devices.

00:32:46.870 --> 00:32:49.540
Are there ways to
lower these costs?

00:32:49.540 --> 00:32:51.100
So yes, it's subdollar.

00:32:51.100 --> 00:32:53.080
And it depends on the
volume, obviously.

00:32:53.080 --> 00:32:56.350
But it's not over a dollar.

00:32:56.350 --> 00:32:57.160
Depends on volume.

00:32:57.160 --> 00:33:03.520
So I would say if you're looking
at low-cost devices, people

00:33:03.520 --> 00:33:09.100
that have bombs that are
maybe $20, $30 or even less,

00:33:09.100 --> 00:33:11.260
that means you're already
looking, oftentimes,

00:33:11.260 --> 00:33:12.970
at fairly high volumes.

00:33:12.970 --> 00:33:15.410
Because otherwise, you
can't get those bombs down.

00:33:15.410 --> 00:33:18.576
And that means that you're going
to get volume discounts, as

00:33:18.576 --> 00:33:19.450
well, on those chips.

00:33:19.450 --> 00:33:21.044
So you'll be way
lower than a dollar.

00:33:21.044 --> 00:33:22.210
So that's one thing to know.

00:33:22.210 --> 00:33:25.000
If you have volume,
subdollar is a guidance.

00:33:25.000 --> 00:33:28.134
But you'll get volume
discounts on that.

00:33:28.134 --> 00:33:30.300
The other thing is you have
to think about the costs

00:33:30.300 --> 00:33:31.110
holistically.

00:33:31.110 --> 00:33:33.030
It's not just the
cost of the bomb.

00:33:33.030 --> 00:33:35.820
It's what it saves you
in manufacturing costs,

00:33:35.820 --> 00:33:42.000
in maintenance, in reputation
cost or dollar amount lost

00:33:42.000 --> 00:33:43.980
through a hack
that could happen,

00:33:43.980 --> 00:33:45.960
and the ease of
maintaining that over time.

00:33:45.960 --> 00:33:49.150
There's a ton of costs
maintaining things over time,

00:33:49.150 --> 00:33:51.010
maintaining things
secure over time,

00:33:51.010 --> 00:33:54.530
that this little crypto
subdollar will solve for you.

00:33:54.530 --> 00:33:58.830
And in my opinion, it is
well worth it to spend that.

00:33:58.830 --> 00:34:01.000
The bomb will be a little
higher for your devices.

00:34:01.000 --> 00:34:04.320
But it's so much cheaper
overall, holistically,

00:34:04.320 --> 00:34:11.219
than you can really
see or get without it.

00:34:11.219 --> 00:34:13.530
Are there other questions
than this coming up?

00:34:13.530 --> 00:34:14.290
OK.

00:34:14.290 --> 00:34:16.810
So I mean, well, stay
tuned for the next session.

00:34:16.810 --> 00:34:20.050
We have a [INAUDIBLE] chat,
efficiently migrating data

00:34:20.050 --> 00:34:22.469
into the Google
Cloud Platform, which

00:34:22.469 --> 00:34:23.620
will be pretty interesting.

00:34:23.620 --> 00:34:25.300
Thank you.

00:34:25.300 --> 00:34:28.650
[MUSIC PLAYING]

